    "text": "Skip to content\nWittgenstein, played\nThe path\nSources\nNotebook\n\n1\n\n\u201cPhilosophy is not a theory but an activity.\u201d\nTRACTATUS 4.112\nWittgenstein, played\n\nSeven of Ludwig Wittgenstein's ideas, from his first book to his last notes. Each one is a short game: you do the thing the idea is about, then read what he wrote.\n\nContinue: 7. Hinges of certainty\nRead the sources\n\nFree. No account: your progress stays in this browser.\n\nThe path, early to late \u00b7 start anywhere\n1\nPictures of facts\nTractatus\nA sentence pictures a possible fact: true if things stand as it shows, false if not.\n2\nThe builders\nInvestigations \u00a7\u00a71\u201321\nWords get their meaning inside an activity: four words can be a complete language.\n3\nMeaning is use\nInvestigations \u00a7\u00a723\u201343\nFor a large class of cases, the meaning of a word is its use in the language.\n4\nFamily resemblance\nInvestigations \u00a7\u00a765\u201377\nMany concepts hang together by overlapping similarities, like a family, not by one shared essence.\n5\nFollowing a rule\nInvestigations \u00a7\u00a7185\u2013242\nExamples and explanations never fix a rule on their own; following a rule is a practice.\nDone\n6\nThe beetle in the box\nInvestigations \u00a7\u00a7243\u2013315\nWords for sensations do not get their meaning by naming private objects.\nDone\n7\nHinges of certainty\nOn Certainty\nOur doubts and checks turn on certainties that stand fast, like hinges; and what stands fast can shift.\n8/9\nHow each game works\n\n1\n\nPlay\n\nYou do the thing the idea is about: fetch stones for a builder, set up a stage, teach a pupil a rule, talk about beetles in boxes.\n\n2\n\nRead\n\nEach session ends with the passage it comes from, quoted with its section number, and a plain reading of it.\n\n3\n\nSee where it stops\n\nEvery game is an analogy. We say where it stops, so the game never stands in for what Wittgenstein wrote.\n\nSources\nYour notebook\nQuotations: Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (tr. Ogden), Philosophical Investigations (tr. Anscombe), On Certainty (tr. Paul & Anscombe). Illustrations made with gpt-image-2.",
