    "text": "Skip to content\n7. Hinges of certainty\n\nThe river-bed \u00b7 On Certainty\n\n1\nDoubt it\nThe river-bed\n\n2 of 4 right first time\n\nOn Certainty \u00a796\n\n\u201cIt might be imagined that some propositions, of the form of empirical propositions, were hardened and functioned as channels for such empirical propositions as were not hardened but fluid; and that this relation altered with time, in that fluid propositions hardened, and hard ones became fluid.\u201d\n\nHinges look like ordinary statements of fact; what makes them hinges is the role they play, and that role can change.\n\nDONE \u00b7 THE RIVER-BED\n\nWhat stands fast can shift over time: hard propositions become fluid, fluid ones harden (\u00a7\u00a796\u201397).\nNext idea: 1. Pictures of facts\nPlay again\nRound by round\nWhat stands fast can shift: in 1969 a hard certainty became open to checking, then false (\u00a7\u00a796\u201397, 106).\nYou: Sorted the river-bed in 1950 and 1970\nOur arguments flow along a bed of things not argued about.\nYou: The questions we argue about and settle.\nWe check things against a background that we did not check.\nYou: No: it came with the picture of the world I was given, the background I check things against.\nHinges are not unchecked assumptions; without them nothing could be checked.\nYou: No: if everything were up for checking, nothing could be checked.\nMore from Wittgenstein\n\u2304\nAbout this game\nHow to play\nEach session starts with something to do: check a claim and then what the check takes for granted, or sort claims into the water and the river-bed for two different years.\nThen answer questions about what you saw. A wrong answer gets a reply explaining why; try again.\nEach round ends with the point it shows, and each session with the passage it comes from.\nThe idea\n\nDoubting needs something it does not doubt, as a door needs hinges that stay put.\n\nOn Certainty \u00a7341. Read the passage",
