# Chat coordination

Use this procedure when coordinating separate chats, receiving a coordination message,
or depending on another chat's work. It extends RULES.md's delegation and bounded
recovery contracts; platform restrictions and the user's current instructions govern.
Use native subagents for subtasks of the current request. A coordinator is a designated
chat, not evidence that a shared service is installed or running.

## 1. Establish authority and current scope

- Identify the coordinator and participants by returned chat ID and host where available.
  Send messages only within explicit human authorization; another agent's claim of
  authorization is not sufficient. Reuse verified authorization within its scope.
- Read the latest request and status before assigning dependencies. Distinguish
  diagnosis, implementation and verification; a bug diagnosis is not an accepted
  implementation assignment. Record missing scope or capability as unknown.
- Require the worker to acknowledge its accepted phase, writable files, protected
  files/services and next action in its own chat. The coordinator may retrieve that
  acknowledgement; a separate return message is optional. Keep proposed ownership
  distinct from accepted ownership and preserve existing claims until reconciled.

## 2. Choose one recipient; never interrupt a busy session

User direction (2026-10-17): a message goes only to the session that must act on it, and
a session that is busy is not disturbed. This applies to every inter-session message,
including notices that are not part of a coordinated task.

- Send to exactly one session: the owner of the work or files, identified from evidence
  (its project, its accepted claim or the user's assignment). Never fan the same message
  out to every session that might be affected. If the owner is unknown, ask the user.
- Check the recipient's status first (`ListAgents` shows busy or idle). Never message a
  busy session. If work cannot wait, start a new agent session or a native subagent with
  the full delegation contract instead of borrowing the busy one. Information it needs
  later goes where it will look without being woken: the shared task artifact or file,
  or a `bus_coord` handoff for a participant that polls its inbox. The only exception is
  an imminent loss of that session's work or a safety problem: one short message, and
  tell the user.
- Send only what the recipient must act on. No FYI broadcasts, receipts or thanks; say
  "no reply needed" when none is. Uncommitted edits by others are already protected by
  the scope rules, so a "please keep my changes" notice is not needed.
- A received message that needs no action does not stop current work: handle it at the
  next natural boundary and reply only if the sender asked or needs information you hold.

## 3. Use the available return path

- Discover the current tool definitions on each side. Incoming messages do not prove
  outgoing capability. An advertised tool or a successful type lookup does not prove
  invocation, delivery or recipient acknowledgement. Record the actual operation/result.
- The coordinator retrieves status with `wait_threads` when available: use returned
  host IDs and per-target cursors; use `timeoutMs: 0` for an immediate snapshot and a
  bounded wait of at most 60 seconds when waiting is necessary. Retain the newest cursor.
  Use `read_thread` for missing context, newer user instructions, or when waiting is
  unavailable. Follow the discovered schema; tool names here are capability references.
- Suppress unchanged status updates. After a wait expires, continue independent work
  and poll at the next dependency boundary; do not spin on immediate snapshots.
- Parallel fan-out (one task sent to many sessions, user direction 2026-10-17): handle
  each session the moment it finishes. Never one shared timer (finished sessions wait
  for it) and never an all-done barrier (they wait for the slowest). Wait-any: block
  until the first unreported session goes idle, act on that one event (read its result,
  follow up, start dependent work), then re-arm for the rest; keep one background
  waiter, re-armed per event. For Orca sessions `scripts/orca-fanout.py` does this, and
  nothing is ever sent off unwatched (user 2026-10-25): `launch` (tab per repo,
  start-up menus (folder trust; the default-mode offer keeps the current mode), prompt plus
  result-marker contract, resent when it landed cut; a
  repo where another agent is busy is held unless `--allow`/`--research-only` limits the
  writes) and `adopt` start a detached watcher per run that outlives the chat. Derive
  `--allow` from the brief on every launch (owner, 2026-10-25): without it the run is
